/* Large-initialized-.data regression corpus for the dynamic PE
   section-layout fix. The committed differential net had no large
   `.data` case (the other oracles' `.data` is only an 8-byte string
   pointer). `blob` is a single >8 KB string literal: a file-scope
   `static const char[]` whose every byte is a real initialized image
   byte (mdbcc emits the whole array into `.data`; nothing is
   zero-fill/.bss), so this forces a `.data` section far past the old
   4 KB ceiling and exercises the next-section-after-a-big-one layout
   math. main walks blob to its terminating NUL (the literal is pure
   printable ASCII -- no embedded NUL), folding every byte into an
   unsigned 16-bit rolling checksum plus a length count, then prints
   both. The whole object is consumed at runtime, and the specific
   target toolchains here (`cl /O2`, `bcc32 5.5.1 -O`, and mdbcc,
   which has no constant-folding pass) do not dead-strip it -- but a
   sufficiently strong optimizer would be entitled to fold the
   checksum to a constant and strip the array (its bytes are pure
   compile-time constants). The large-`.data` regime is therefore
   locked durably and optimizer-independently by a structural PE
   assertion in `tests/pe_layout.rs`
   (`large_data_section_is_structurally_initialized_and_loads`), not
   by this stdout checksum alone. Targets cl/bcc32/mdbcc, not strict
   ISO C89: the single ~10.6 KB string literal far exceeds C89's
   509-char string-literal minimum (all three target compilers accept
   it; the object stays within C89's 32767-byte limit). The rest is
   strict: decls before stmts; only slash-star comments; no C99/VLA/
   long long/stdint/sizeof/%p/time/rand. `int` is 32-bit on cl,
   bcc32 (Win32) and mdbcc; all
   arithmetic is unsigned (defined wrap) and masked to 16 bits, so
   stdout is byte-identical across all three regardless of pointer
   size. Deterministic; ends with return 0;. */
